call_details.response:
The board state shows that player o has three winning cells at (1,0), (1,4) and (0,4). The cells at (1,4) and (0,4) lie in column 4 and can be converted into a forced win for o by the following sequence (provided column 4 is left open and the cell at (0,1) is also left open):

- o drops in column 4, occupying (3,4) and thereby creating an immediate threat on the diagonal whose empty cell is (0,1);
- x is forced to answer in column 1;
- o drops again in column 4, occupying (2,4) and thereby unlocking the already-prepared horizontal threat at (1,4);
- x is forced to answer at (1,4);
- o drops a third time in column 4, occupying (0,4) and completing the diagonal (0,4)–(1,3)–(2,2)–(3,1).

Exactly the same forced win appears if x is the first player to occupy (3,4). Consequently every opening move except column 1 loses: any other legal column leaves both (3,4) and (0,1) free, after which o simply executes the sequence above.

Playing column 1 occupies the critical cell (0,1). That single stone has two decisive effects:

- the diagonal threat that would have been created by an o-stone at (3,4) is permanently blocked;
- if o nevertheless occupies (3,4), the cell (2,4) becomes the lowest empty square of column 4 and is already a winning cell for x (the diagonal (5,1)–(4,2)–(3,3)–(2,4)). x would therefore win on the next move.

Thus o is prevented from ever starting the column-4 climb. The only other winning cell of o, (1,0), requires three moves to unlock and never generates a double threat, so it cannot force a win against correct defence. With the unique forced loss eliminated, column 1 is the sole optimal opening.

Final Answer: 1

